<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ruhr</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ruhr area, Ruhrgebiet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ruhr is an urban and industrial region in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, named after the river Ruhr, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> that forms its southern edge. From the 19th century its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mines and steelworks, in cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a>, Dortmund, Duisburg and Bochum, made it the industrial heartland of Germany. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> occupied it in 1923 to enforce the payment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ruhr.jpg" alt="Ruhr" width="320" height="379" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_map_RVR_in_Germany.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Ruhr area, or Ruhrgebiet, is a polycentric urban area bordered by the river Ruhr to the south, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> to the west and the Lippe to the north. Its cities lie, from west to east, along a line running through Duisburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a>, Bochum and Dortmund, and the area has no administrative centre: each city has its own administration. Geologically the region is defined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>-bearing layers of the upper Carboniferous period. The coal seams reach the surface in a strip along the river Ruhr and dip downward towards the north, lying 600 to 800 metres deep beneath the Lippe, which largely determined the development of coal mining.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> much of the region lay in the County of Mark, the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, the State of Berg and the territories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Münster and the archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. It was mainly agrarian. The free imperial city of Dortmund was its trading and cultural centre on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellweg">Hellweg</a>, an important east–west trade route that also brought prosperity to Duisburg.</p><p>Industrialisation began in the late 18th century in the nearby Wupper Valley in the Bergisches Land. From 1807 to 1813 the area was part of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, a client state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s brother Jérôme, and by about 1820 hundreds of water-powered mills produced textiles and iron. As machines moved from water to steam, the Bergische industry ordered more and more <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> from the new mining area along the Ruhr, and railways brought coal and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> from the Ruhr into the hills.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapp-putsch">Kapp Putsch</a> of March 1920, which a general strike defeated, an armed revolt in the Ruhr aimed to replace the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> with a soviet-style council republic. The Ruhr Red Army took control of the industrial area, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichswehr">Reichswehr</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> units put the rising down in early April 1920; an estimated 1,000 insurgents and 200 soldiers were killed. In 1923 French and Belgian troops occupied the district and seized its main industrial assets in lieu of unpaid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the Ruhr Authority was created to regulate its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> industries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Ruhr is the largest urban area in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and part of the larger Rhine-Ruhr metropolitan region. Since the 1980s it has been the target of financial support from the state, the federal government and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>. A supracommunal Ruhr Regional Association has its seat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ruhr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Today the republic covers 357,022 square kilometres and has 84.7 million people by the 2023 census, the most populous member state of the European Union; Berlin is the largest city and the <b>Ruhr</b> the largest urban area.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Early violence was heavy: fighting with the People's Navy Division in December 1918 was followed by the Spartacist uprising of 5–12 January 1919, with an estimated 150 to 200 dead, strikes of 350,000 <b>Ruhr</b> miners and the first Silesian uprising of August 1919, put down by military means.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-state-of-prussia">Free State of Prussia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Railways spread rapidly after the line from Nuremberg to Fürth opened in 1835 and the Leipzig–Dresden line followed in 1839, and the Rhineland, the <b>Ruhr</b>, Saxony and Silesia began to industrialise.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Rhineland and Westphalia, gained in 1815, made Prussia an industrial power, with coal and iron in the <b>Ruhr</b> and Upper Silesia, firms such as Krupp, a railway network from the 1840s and the Zollverein customs union, launched under Prussian leadership in 1834, which bound much of the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The capture of the Klaipėda Region, detached from East Prussia, during the <b>Ruhr</b> Crisis of 1923, and its autonomy within Lithuania from May 1924, set it against Germany, whose inhabitants of the region resisted Lithuanian rule in the 1930s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Printing money to pay for passive resistance to the occupation of the <b>Ruhr</b> drove the hyperinflation of 1923: savings became worthless, while debtors and large concerns such as those of Hugo Stinnes, the Inflation King, gained.</blockquote><a href="/empire/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
